---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../components/PageHeader.astro';
import { site, toolkitReady, dcProduct, dcReady } from '../../data/site';
import EmailCapture from '../../components/EmailCapture.astro';
const crumbs = [{ name: 'Toolkit', href: '/toolkit/' }];
---
<Base
  title={`${site.toolkitName}: Excel Workbook | Ohmetry`}
  description="A fifteen-sheet Excel workbook: IEC and NEC circuit schedule, linked panel schedule, relay grading with plot, fault level with cable impedance, and self-checks."
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
  noindex={!toolkitReady}
>
  <PageHeader title={site.toolkitName} lead="The calculators here give you one answer at a time. The toolkit checks a whole project: circuit and load schedules, relay grading with a coordination plot, fault levels and more, in a workbook you keep." eyebrow="Workbook" crumbs={crumbs} />
  <article class="article prose">
    <h2>What is inside</h2>
    <p>One workbook, fifteen sheets, no macros. Every result is a formula you can read.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Circuit Schedule.</strong> Up to 30 circuits in one table, each checked to the IEC method or the NEC method. For each one it works out the load current, applies the 125 % rule for NEC continuous loads, picks the next standard protective device, compares it with the cable rating you enter, and calculates voltage drop. Each row ends in PASS or CHECK.</li>
      <li><strong>Panel Schedule.</strong> Linked to the Circuit Schedule: every circuit appears with a demand factor and a phase. It gives connected and demand load, the supply current, the minimum main device, the load on each phase and the imbalance.</li>
      <li><strong>Load Schedule.</strong> A separate estimate by load type: connected load, demand load with your own demand factors, a growth allowance, the supply current and the minimum main device.</li>
      <li><strong>Relay Settings.</strong> Operating time of three IDMT overcurrent relays on the four IEC 60255-151 curves, a grading-margin check between each pair, and a log-log coordination plot.</li>
      <li><strong>Fault with Cable.</strong> Maximum and minimum fault level and peak current at the end of a cable, adding the source, transformer and cable impedances, with a breaker check.</li>
      <li><strong>Seven calculator sheets.</strong> Power &amp; Current, Cable by Drop, Transformer &amp; Faults, Power Factor, CT &amp; Earthing, Generator, and Solar &amp; Battery.</li>
      <li><strong>Self-Test.</strong> Each calculator holds a worked example whose expected result was calculated separately. The sheet shows 83 checks and whether they pass.</li>
      <li><strong>Lists.</strong> IEC and NEC device ratings, conductor resistivity, curve constants and cable sizes, all editable to match your standard.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What it does not contain</h2>
    <p>Cable current ratings, derating factors, demand factors and code tables. You enter the tabulated rating and factors from the standard you work to, whether that is IEC 60364-5-52 or the NEC, and the workbook checks your design against them. That keeps the workbook usable under either system, and it means the numbers that decide a cable's size come from the code you hold, not from a copy inside a spreadsheet.</p>

    <h2>Who it is for</h2>
    <p>Electrical, solar and protection engineers who check designs in a spreadsheet and want to see the formula behind every number. It is written by a protection engineer with MV/HV experience under IEC practice. The formulas are the ones explained in the guides on this site.</p>

    <h2>Free calculators and the workbook</h2>
    <p>The calculators on this site stay free. Use them for a quick answer. The workbook is for checking a whole project at once, working offline and adapting the method. It was built and checked in LibreOffice, its formula types were checked in Google Sheets, and it is designed to open in Excel. Start with the guides on <a href="/voltage-drop-formula/">voltage drop</a> and the <a href="/relay-setting-checklist/">relay setting checklist</a> to see how the calculations are explained.</p>

    {dcReady && (
      <>
        <h2>Also: {dcProduct.name}</h2>
        <p><img src="/images/dc-sample-cover.png" alt="Single-line diagram and title of the 2 MW data centre electrical sample project" width="1280" height="720" loading="lazy" style="max-width:100%;height:auto" /></p>
        <p>A worked example for engineers who work on data centre projects. It is a <strong>sample and learning reference, not a real project</strong> and not a design for any site. Every number is a labelled assumption, so you can see how each one drives the result and then replace it with your own project data.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Twenty Excel workbooks</strong> for a 2 MW IT load: load estimate and PUE with a sensitivity table, transformer, generator and UPS sizing with options side by side, battery energy, cable sizing, voltage drop, short circuit, switchgear, busduct, earthing screening and an equipment schedule.</li>
          <li><strong>A single-line diagram</strong> of a 2N, N+1 concept. Bypass, interlocking and the power management system are shown as labelled concept symbols, not as a design.</li>
          <li><strong>Ten specification templates</strong> with vendor data and compliance schedules, FAT, SAT and commissioning sections, and a twelve-module training suite with exercises and an answer key.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Every input shows whether it is missing, a placeholder, an assumption, supplied or verified. A result calculated from unverified inputs never reads PASS, and a warning is never turned into a pass.</p>
        <h3>What it is not</h3>
        <p>It is not construction-ready, not independently verified and not approved by anyone. It does not claim compliance with any standard. Arc flash and lightning protection are out of scope, and protection coordination is a template only. Do not use any result for procurement, construction or safety decisions without manufacturer data and verification by a qualified engineer.</p>
        <p><a class="btn" href={dcProduct.gumroadUrl}>Get the sample project for {dcProduct.price}</a></p>
        <p class="meta">One-time payment through Gumroad. You download one ZIP file with a README. Questions: hello@ohmetry.com.</p>
      </>
    )}

    {toolkitReady ? (
      <>
        <h2>Get the toolkit</h2>
        <p><a class="btn" href={site.gumroadUrl}>Get the toolkit for {site.price}</a></p>
        <p class="meta">One-time payment through Gumroad. You download the Excel workbook straight away.</p>
        <EmailCapture heading="Not ready to buy? Get the free sample sheet" />
        <p class="meta">See the <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> for what is collected and how it is used.</p>
      </>
    ) : (
      <p class="meta">This page is a draft: the Gumroad link has not been set yet (see <code>src/data/site.ts</code>). It is hidden from search engines until they are.</p>
    )}
  </article>
</Base>
